Let a STATIC libMNN register its Vulkan backend.

MNN registers each optional backend from a file-scope initializer -- here a
`gResistor` in VulkanRuntime.cpp that calls MNNInsertExtraRuntimeCreator. An
initializer only runs if the linker pulls its object out of the archive, and
nothing else in MNN references anything in that file.

With MNN_SEP_BUILD=OFF and a static libMNN, which is what an addon that has to
be one .node needs, the object is therefore dropped. The build compiles the
whole Vulkan backend, links it into MNN.lib, and the runtime then reports

    Can't Find type=7 backend, use 0 instead

and runs on the CPU. Nothing else says anything is wrong: the model loads, the
answer is correct, and only the timings are the CPU's. A shared libMNN does not
have the problem, which is why upstream does not.

This adds one extern "C" function for a host to reference. It does nothing on
purpose -- the registration stays in the initializer, so a shared build is
unchanged -- and referencing it is what drags the object into the link.

Upstream solves the same problem for CUDA with /WHOLEARCHIVE on the MNN target
(CMakeLists.txt:925), which is not available to a consumer of the archive
without pulling all 298 MB of it in.

diff --git a/source/backend/vulkan/runtime/VulkanRuntime.cpp b/source/backend/vulkan/runtime/VulkanRuntime.cpp
index 676895f..8e52117 100644
--- a/source/backend/vulkan/runtime/VulkanRuntime.cpp
+++ b/source/backend/vulkan/runtime/VulkanRuntime.cpp
@@ -292,3 +292,20 @@ static bool gResistor = []() {
     return false;
 }();
 }
+
+// A symbol to reference, for a libMNN linked as a STATIC library.
+//
+// The creator above registers itself from a file-scope initializer, and an
+// initializer only runs if the linker pulls its object out of the archive.
+// Nothing else in MNN references anything in this file, so with
+// MNN_SEP_BUILD=OFF and a static libMNN the object is never pulled in, the
+// creator is never registered, and a build that compiled Vulkan perfectly
+// reports "Can't Find type=7 backend, use 0 instead" and silently runs on the
+// CPU. A shared libMNN does not have the problem: every object is in the .so.
+//
+// Calling this from the host is enough -- one referenced symbol drags the whole
+// object in, initializer included. It does nothing itself, deliberately: the
+// registration must stay where it is so a shared build keeps working unchanged.
+extern "C" void MNNForceLinkVulkanRuntime() {
+    (void)MNN::gResistor;
+}
